Confidence calibration is essential to guaranteeing reliable machine learning decisions. However, deep neural networks typically exhibit a propensity for harsh penalties and exhibit poor calibration. Although numerous studies have addressed this issue in controlled laboratory settings, there is evidence suggesting that calibrated outputs are unlikely to arise solely from adequate training on regulated benchmarks. We contend that reliable classification at test time necessitates reflecting the model's lack of certainty when dealing with inputs outside its trained distribution. To achieve this level of reliability in such scenarios, we must account for the probability of encounters with open-world uncertainty, which includes outputs that are neither classified nor confirmed as belonging to any known category but rather remain unassigned. To achieve this, we present an open-world uncertainty modeling method that utilizes an auxiliary energy model trained exclusively on the limited closed-world labeled dataset at hand but capable of reflecting both the likelihood of encountering open-world instances and the level of uncertainty associated with those encounters while maintaining proper calibration. Experimental results indicate that this technique can effectively reflect this uncertainty and ensure reliable classification outcomes.
